Confirmed: `Ecosystem` has no `Display` impl. Its only string form is `prefix()` returning `"cargo"`, `"npm"`, etc. That makes the message-content assertion in AC-5 genuinely ambiguous.

Here is the audit result:

```json
{
  "issues": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-4",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "\"The [[package-set]] fallback is still consulted\" describes internal lookup behavior, not an observable outcome. \"Consulted\" admits two interpretations: (1) the code merely invokes the package-set lookup path, or (2) when a package-set rule matches, its PackageConfig is what the caller receives. An implementer can satisfy interpretation (1) by calling the lookup and discarding the result. Only interpretation (2) is verifiable from outside.",
      "suggested_fix": "Rewrite as: \"When no [[package]] rule matches a package and a [[package-set]] rule does match, the [[package-set]] rule's PackageConfig is returned. When neither matches, None is returned. This behavior is unchanged by this spec.\""
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "\"A message containing ... the matched ecosystem names\" does not specify what string representation to use for ecosystem values. The Ecosystem type has no Display impl; its only string form is Ecosystem::prefix() which returns \"cargo\" and \"npm\". An implementer could also use the Rust enum variant names (\"Cargo\", \"Npm\"), a fully qualified form, or any other rendering. A test cannot write a substring assertion without knowing which form is required.",
      "suggested_fix": "Rewrite the message clause as: \"a message containing the pattern name (the bare-pattern Display string, e.g. \\\"foo\\\") and the prefix strings of the matched ecosystems as returned by Ecosystem::prefix() (e.g. \\\"cargo\\\", \\\"npm\\\") — at minimum each prefix string must appear as a distinct substring of the message.\""
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "AC-4 uses the internal-behavior verb 'consulted' where an observable return-value assertion is needed; a test cannot verify whether a lookup path was taken, only what was returned. AC-5's message-content requirement is untestable as written because Ecosystem has no Display impl and the spec does not name Ecosystem::prefix() as the required string form, leaving the implementer unable to write a substring-match assertion without a code-archaeology detour. Both issues are fixable with targeted rewrites and do not require rethinking the design. All other criteria (AC-1 through AC-3, AC-6 through AC-9) are specific, observable, and testable as written."
}
```

