      "criterion_id": "AC-3",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The phrase 'cfg.packages contains only Prefixed PackageId rules that match the same package' is ambiguous about what 'only' scopes to. Interpretation A: cfg.packages has no Bare entries at all — the entire Vec is Prefixed. Interpretation B: among all rules in cfg.packages that match this specific package, all happen to be Prefixed — unmatching Bare entries may still be present in the Vec. The distinction determines the test fixture a developer constructs. Under (A) the test has cfg.packages = [Prefixed_A, Prefixed_B]; under (B) the test may also contain Bare entries that do not match the package under test. Given that AC-1, AC-2, and AC-3 together are meant to partition the matching-rule-type space, interpretation (B) is almost certainly correct, but the phrasing does not make this unambiguous.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace the AC-3 description with: 'When the set of [[package]] rules in cfg.packages that match a given package consists entirely of Prefixed PackageId rules (no Bare rule in cfg.packages matches that package — other Bare rules that match different packages may still be present), the first-matching Prefixed rule in declaration order is applied to that package.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The phrase 'for each matched ecosystem, its Ecosystem::prefix() value ... as a distinct substring' leaves 'distinct' undefined. It is unclear whether 'distinct' means (a) each prefix occurs as at least one contiguous character sequence somewhere in the message, or (b) each prefix's occurrence must be non-overlapping with other prefixes' occurrences. Because the current Ecosystem prefix set has no string that is a prefix of another (the set is: cargo, npm, pypi, go, maven, nuget, deno, jsr), this ambiguity is harmless in practice for today's test cases, but the word 'distinct' is unexplained and will confuse an implementer writing the assertion. Additionally the message format is otherwise unconstrained: an implementer cannot know whether the prefixes must appear in a comma-separated list, a structured sentence, or any other layout.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace the message requirement in AC-5 with: 'a message that contains the pattern's name string (the return value of pattern.name()) as a substring, and, for each distinct Ecosystem value whose prefix() was matched, contains that prefix() value as a contiguous character sequence. The message format is otherwise unconstrained. Example acceptable message: \"bare rule \\\"foo\\\" matches packages in multiple ecosystems: cargo, npm\".' Remove the word 'distinct' as a qualifier on 'substring'; it adds no testable meaning and the current prefix set already guarantees non-overlap."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "cfg.packages is typed as Vec<(PackageId, PackageConfig)> (confirmed in crates/callisto-graph/src/config/resolve.rs line 24), which structurally allows two entries with identical Bare PackageId patterns — TOML [[package]] array headers can repeat. No criterion addresses this. The current text 'N qualifying Bare rules produce N diagnostics total' would logically imply that two identical Bare entries produce 2 diagnostics for the same pattern name, but an implementer could also reasonably deduplicate the Vec before the diagnostic pass. The correct behavior is unspecified.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add criterion AC-5b: 'If cfg.packages contains two entries whose PackageId is the same Bare pattern, each entry is treated as an independent rule for diagnostic purposes. If both entries independently qualify (i.e., their PackageId::matches() predicate returns true for packages spanning two or more distinct ecosystems), two Diagnostic values are pushed — one per entry. If deduplication is preferred, add a non-goal: \"Deduplication of identical PackageId entries in cfg.packages is out of scope; the diagnostic pass iterates the Vec as-is.\"'"
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "The spec is well-scoped, has no TBDs, and most criteria are precisely testable. Two issues block a pass verdict. First, AC-3's use of 'only' is genuinely ambiguous about whether it scopes to the entire cfg.packages Vec or only to the matching subset; this directly affects what test fixture a developer constructs and produces two equally valid but divergent implementations. Second, the Vec<(PackageId, PackageConfig)> backing type allows structural duplicates, and the behavior for duplicate Bare entries is unspecified — 'N qualifying Bare rules produce N diagnostics' does not resolve whether a duplicated entry counts as 1 rule or 2. The AC-5 'distinct substring' wording is a lower-severity ambiguity that should be cleaned up but would not in isolation block implementation given the non-overlapping current prefix set."
}
```

