```json
{
  "issues": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-2",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The phrase 'cfg.packages contains only Bare PackageId rules that match the same package' admits two readings: (a) cfg.packages as a whole contains no Prefixed rules at all, or (b) among the rules in cfg.packages that match this specific package, all are Bare — while Prefixed rules for other packages may still exist in the Vec. Reading (a) is the more natural grammatical parse ('contains only Bare… rules'). Reading (b) is what the two-pass algorithm produces. AC-3 has an explicit clarifying parenthetical for its analogous condition; AC-2 does not.",
      "suggested_fix": "Rewrite as: 'When the set of [[package]] rules in cfg.packages that match a given package consists entirely of Bare PackageId rules (no Prefixed rule in cfg.packages matches that package — other Prefixed rules matching different packages may still be present in the Vec), the first-matching Bare rule in declaration order is applied to that package (existing behavior, unchanged).'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-4a",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "AC-4a says 'a [[package-set]] rule does match' (singular). When multiple [[package-set]] rules match the same package, which PackageConfig is returned? The implementation uses .find(), which is first-match-wins, but this is not stated in the criterion. A developer writing a test for this boundary could produce a test that passes with the wrong behavior.",
      "suggested_fix": "Append to the criterion: 'When multiple [[package-set]] rules match the same package, the first matching rule in cfg.package_sets declaration order is applied (first-match-wins, consistent with the existing .find() call at that tier).'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "AC-5 says 'collect the distinct ecosystems of all packages in the just-built packages map whose id is matched by pattern.' The packages map is keyed on PackageId values. If any PackageId key in the map is Bare (ecosystem() returns None), there is no Ecosystem value to collect — yet the spec says to count distinct Ecosystem values. The spec does not state whether: (1) Bare-keyed entries are skipped, (2) they count as a distinct non-ecosystem bucket, or (3) the invariant holds that all map keys are always Prefixed. A developer who does not know the locator's invariants cannot implement this without a question.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add a sentence after 'whose id is matched by pattern': 'Only packages whose PackageId key in the map is Prefixed (ecosystem() returns Some) are considered; Bare-keyed packages (ecosystem() returns None) are excluded from the ecosystem set and do not contribute to the distinct-ecosystem count. (In practice the locator always produces Prefixed ids, so this exclusion is a no-op, but the implementation must not panic or unwrap on a Bare key.)'"
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "Two issues block a clean implementation without clarifying questions. AC-2 has a genuine grammatical ambiguity: the 'only Bare rules' qualifier could be read as applying to cfg.packages globally (no Prefixed rules at all) or only to the rules that match the target package. A developer who reads it globally will write a test that passes under the wrong condition and leave the Prefixed-shadows-Bare case untested for AC-2. AC-5 has an unspecified edge case: when a package in the map carries a Bare PackageId key, ecosystem() returns None and there is no Ecosystem value to add to the distinct set; the spec gives no rule for handling this. AC-4a is a lesser gap (multi-match behavior for package-sets is undefined) but is not blocking because the existing .find() semantics are inferable from the surrounding code."
}
```

