{
  "issues": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-1",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The phrase 'at least one Prefixed PackageId rule (ecosystem() is Some) and at least one Bare PackageId rule (ecosystem() is None) that all match the same package' has an ambiguous antecedent for 'that all'. Grammatically, 'that all' could modify (a) only the Bare rules — one or more Bare rules each matching the same package — or (b) both tiers together — a Prefixed rule and a Bare rule that together match the same package. A developer writing a test fixture must pick one interpretation to decide whether the precondition requires both rule types to match, or only the Bare rules to match. The intended reading appears to be (b), but the phrasing does not enforce it.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace: '...at least one Prefixed PackageId rule (ecosystem() is Some) and at least one Bare PackageId rule (ecosystem() is None) that all match the same package...' with: '...at least one Prefixed PackageId rule (ecosystem() is Some) that matches a given package P and at least one Bare PackageId rule (ecosystem() is None) that also matches that same package P...'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The term 'packages map produced by the current ManifestWalkResolver::build invocation' is used five times in AC-5 but is never defined in the acceptance criteria. Its key type, value type, and relationship to cfg.packages are not stated. A developer writing a test for AC-5 must infer from context outside the ACs (the api_surface section or the existing function signature) that this is the output map being assembled during build, keyed by PackageId. Two developers could write incompatible test fixtures if they infer different structures — for example, whether a 'package with a Bare PackageId key' is a key present in this output map or an input package being resolved.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add one sentence at the start of AC-5 that defines the term: 'The packages map referred to below is the map of (PackageId, PackageConfig) pairs assembled during the current ManifestWalkResolver::build invocation — that is, the output collection populated by the packages loop, where each key is the PackageId of a discovered package and each value is its resolved PackageConfig.' Then replace all subsequent occurrences of 'the packages map produced by the current ManifestWalkResolver::build invocation' with 'the packages map'."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": null,
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "No AC explicitly covers the case where cfg.packages is non-empty (contains at least one Bare rule) but the output packages map is empty — that is, the walk discovers zero packages. In this scenario the diagnostic pass iterates Bare rules that match zero packages, so the Prefixed-keyed ecosystem count is 0 for every rule. The correct behavior (no diagnostic, per the combination of AC-5 and AC-6) is only derivable by reading two criteria together; it is never stated as a standalone postcondition. A developer writing a regression test for an empty workspace would have no single AC to cite as the oracle.",
      "suggested_fix": "Extend AC-9 to cover this case: 'When cfg.packages is empty, no diagnostic is pushed and the [[package]]-tier lookup returns None for every queried package. Likewise, when cfg.packages is non-empty but the output packages map contains zero entries (no packages were discovered during the walk), no diagnostic is pushed (each Bare rule has a Prefixed-keyed ecosystem count of zero, satisfying AC-6), and the [[package]]-tier lookup returns None for any package queried. In both cases the [[package-set]]-tier fallback (AC-4a) continues to apply normally.'"
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "Two issues require a developer to make an interpretive choice before writing tests. AC-1's grammatical ambiguity in 'that all match the same package' admits two distinct test-fixture designs depending on whether 'all' scopes over both tiers or only the Bare tier. AC-5 uses the term 'packages map' as a load-bearing concept throughout its most complex criterion but never defines its key/value structure in the ACs, forcing the implementer to consult the api_surface section that the audit is instructed to ignore. The missing-error-case finding is lower severity (derivable by combining AC-5 and AC-6) but introduces a gap in explicit test oracle coverage for the empty-workspace scenario. All three issues require targeted rewrites, not judgment calls by the implementer."
}
