{
  "issues": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The phrase 'at least one Prefixed-keyed entry in the packages map that the rule matched' does not state how matching is determined. A developer could interpret 'matched' as (a) passes pattern.matches(key), (b) was previously selected by the two-pass lookup for this package, or (c) is keyed by a PackageId structurally equal to the rule's pattern. Only interpretation (a) is correct, but the criterion does not say so.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace 'for at least one Prefixed-keyed entry in the packages map that the rule matched' with 'for at least one Prefixed-keyed entry (key, _) in the packages map for which pattern.matches(key) returns true'."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "When N > 1 qualifying Bare rules each produce a diagnostic, the order of those N diagnostics in the output collection is not specified. A developer writing a deterministic test (e.g., asserting diagnostics[0].message contains rule X's name and diagnostics[1].message contains rule Y's name) cannot do so without knowing the ordering guarantee.",
      "suggested_fix": "Append to the sentence starting 'N qualifying Bare rules': 'Diagnostics are pushed in the same order that qualifying Bare rules appear in cfg.packages (declaration order, including duplicates), so the Nth diagnostic corresponds to the Nth qualifying Bare rule encountered during iteration.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "incomplete",
      "description": "AC-5 refers to 'the map of discovered packages assembled during ManifestWalkResolver::build' but does not state at what point during the build the diagnostic pass executes — before the build loop, incrementally inside it, or after the loop completes. Because the distinct-ecosystem set can only be accurate once all packages are discovered, the pass must run over the fully-built map. A developer reading only the acceptance criteria would need to ask: does the diagnostic pass see all discovered packages or only those found so far?",
      "suggested_fix": "Begin the criterion with: 'After all packages have been discovered and inserted into the packages map (i.e., after the full packages loop in ManifestWalkResolver::build completes), for each Bare PackageId rule in cfg.packages…' This removes the ambiguity about pass timing without referencing the implementation detail of where the loop boundary falls."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-9",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "In the second scenario ('cfg.packages is non-empty but the packages map contains zero entries'), the sentence 'the [[package]]-tier lookup returns None for any package queried' is vacuously true because no packages are discovered and therefore no lookups occur during the build loop. The phrasing implies a caller could actively issue a lookup and receive None, which could lead a developer to add an unnecessary early-return guard for the empty-map case rather than relying on the naturally empty iteration. The criterion gives two different reasons for the same observable behavior, which may cause a developer to add code that should not exist.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace 'the [[package]]-tier lookup returns None for any package queried' (in the second scenario) with 'the [[package]]-tier lookup is never invoked (no packages are queried), so no [[package]]-tier result is produced for any package; the [[package-set]]-tier fallback (AC-4a) continues to apply normally when other callers query the resolver.'"
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "Three of the four issues are blocking. AC-5 contains two ambiguities that force a developer to ask clarifying questions before writing tests: (1) the matching predicate for the distinct-ecosystem set computation is unstated, leaving three plausible interpretations, and (2) the output ordering of N diagnostics is unspecified, making deterministic multi-diagnostic tests impossible to write correctly. AC-5 also omits the timing guarantee that the diagnostic pass operates on the fully-built packages map, not an in-progress one — a developer must infer this from api_surface rather than the criterion itself, which violates the no-clarifying-questions standard. AC-9's vacuous-truth phrasing is a lower-severity readability issue but could cause a developer to add dead code. All other criteria (AC-1 through AC-4b, AC-6, AC-7, AC-8) are precise, testable, and unambiguous."
}
```

